 "literal": "Like the pale back of the green-legged heron, / the water-lilies in the deep water have also closed now. / Evening has arrived, may you live! / Not only that, it also possesses the night.",
 "translation": "Like the pale back of the green-legged heron, the water-lilies in the deep water have folded their petals now. Evening has arrived—may you live! And it is not alone, for it brings the night along with it.",
 "modern": "பச்சை நிறக் கால்களைக் கொண்ட நாரையின் வெளுத்த முதுகைப் போல, ஆழமான நீரில் உள்ள ஆம்பல் மலர்களும் இப்போது கூம்பிவிட்டன. மாலைப் பொழுது வந்துவிட்டது, நீ வாழ்க! அதுமட்டும் அல்ல, அது தன்னோடு காரிருள் நிறைந்த இரவையும் கூட்டிக் கொண்டு வந்துள்ளது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine speaks to her confidante in deep distress as the day ends. She observes that the water-lilies in the deep pools have closed, looking just like the pale backs of green-legged herons. This visual change signals that evening, the most painful time for separated lovers, has arrived. She addresses the evening with bitter irony, wishing it well, and notes that it does not come alone. It brings with it the dark, lonely night, which will only intensify her grief of separation.",
